codex
**Verdict: Claims 1–4 contain sound results but need fixes. Claim 5 is wrong as a general barrier.** The main unresolved issue is quantitative detection of fruitfulness among the relations the algorithm actually uses.

Throughout, “coprime to everything relevant” must explicitly include
\[
\gcd\!\left(n,f'(m)\prod_i z_i\right)=1.
\]
Otherwise the quotient defining \(\Phi\) need not exist. In semiprime statements, require **distinct** primes \(p,q\).

**1. CORRECT WITH FIXES.**

The homomorphism is correct. Its proof needs no Blum condition—and no odd-degree assumption.

Write \(A_i=a_i-b_i\alpha\). If \(I=S\cap T\), square roots satisfy
\[
\sqrt{\prod_{S\triangle T}z_i}
 =\pm\frac{\sqrt{\prod_Sz_i}\sqrt{\prod_Tz_i}}{\prod_Iz_i},
\]
and the corresponding identity holds in \(K\) for the \(A_i\). Reduction makes the overlap denominators agree. Consequently,
\[
\Phi(S\triangle T)=\Phi(S)\Phi(T)
\]
after quotienting by the global sign.

There are exactly two square roots of a nonzero square in \(K\), irrespective of other roots of unity in \(K\). Thus their choice introduces only the sign already being quotiented out.

The integrality assertion is also valid: the algebraic square root is integral, and
\[
f'(\alpha)\mathcal O_K\subseteq\mathbb Z[\alpha].
\]
For odd \(n\), CRT and the prime-power calculation give
\[
\mu_2(\mathbb Z/n)\cong\{\pm1\}^{\omega(n)}.
\]
The displayed identification with \(\mathbb F_2^{\omega(n)-1}\) is generally **noncanonical**.

For uniform sampling from the **whole** \(K_0\), including zero,
\[
\Pr(\text{fruitful})=1-2^{-\operatorname{rank}\Phi}.
\]
Sampling a basis vector, or an arbitrary nonzero kernel vector, does not have this distribution.

The final sentence about LV is too compressed. Nonvanishing on the full relation space does not establish nonvanishing on the sampled relations or on a further restricted kernel. You need the nonvanishing hypothesis on the space actually sampled, together with its sampling and cost guarantees. LV’s published §7 supplies the Blum character formulation; its Conjecture 7.1 cannot simply be imported unchanged into the non-Blum setting. [Lee–Venkatesan, §7, Theorem 2.3 and Remark 7.3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873)

A concrete warning about additional restrictions appears under A below.

**2. CORRECT WITH FIXES. The general character formula survives.**

For distinct \(p,q\), your maximal \(2\)-power characters do satisfy
\[
\chi_p(-1)=\chi_q(-1)=-1,\qquad \psi(-1)=1.
\]
This last identity is essential: it makes the omitted signs harmless.

Because the two evaluations of each relation agree modulo \(n\),
\[
\psi(z_i)=\psi(A_i),
\]
so \(2^c\mid t_i\).

For \(S\in K_0\), all total exponents in both complete multiplicative bases are even, and the total torsion signs permit square roots. Therefore, with reductions interpreted in the appropriate localization,
\[
\frac{\psi(\sqrt{\prod_Sz_i})}
     {\psi(\sqrt{\prod_SA_i})}
 =\zeta^{\frac12\sum_{i\in S}t_i}
 =(-1)^{\sum_{i\in S}\beta_i}.
\]
The factors \(f'\) cancel. If the root ratio has local signs \((\varepsilon_p,\varepsilon_q)\), then
\[
\psi(\varepsilon_p,\varepsilon_q)=\varepsilon_p\varepsilon_q.
\]
That is precisely the nontrivial character of
\(\mu_2(\mathbb Z/pq)/\{\pm1\}\). Hence your formula follows.

Odd degree matters here because it ensures \(\mu(K)=\{\pm1\}\), so the stated quotient of the \(S\)-unit group is free. Use the **full** \(S\)-unit lattice, not merely principal-ideal valuation coordinates.

Changing lifts by
\[
\tau_\ell\mapsto\tau_\ell+2^c k_\ell,\qquad
\sigma_j\mapsto\sigma_j+2^c h_j
\]
changes \(\beta_i\) by
\[
\sum_\ell k_\ell e_{i\ell}-\sum_jh_jc_{ij}\pmod2.
\]
Thus the claimed ambiguity is exactly harmless on \(K_0\). More generally, any two linear extensions of the same \(\Phi\) differ by a linear function of the exact square classes.

Two qualifications:

- “Explicit” does **not** establish efficient computability. Computing a complete \(S\)-unit basis and relation coordinates requires a separate cost analysis.
- Outside the Blum case, the character construction uses information about the unknown factors. It is an analytic description, not an immediately available factoring subroutine.

For the special cases:

**2a is correct for Blum semiprimes.** Quadratic reciprocity gives
\[
J_n(\ell)=(n/\ell)=1
\]
for every odd prime dividing \(x_i^2-n\), under the coprimality assumptions.

Your expression has a minor well-definedness defect: \(v_2(z_i)/2\) can be nonintegral when its coefficient is zero. A cleaner formula is
\[
\beta_{\rm QS}(x)
=[J_n(x)=-1]+[n\equiv5\pmod8]\,[x\text{ odd}]
\pmod2.
\]
When \(n\equiv5\pmod8\) and \(x\) is odd, \(v_2(x^2-n)=2\).

**It is false outside that scope.** For
\[
n=65,\qquad x=9,\qquad z=16,
\]
the congruence \(9^2\equiv4^2\pmod{65}\) is fruitful, while
\[
J_{65}(9)=J_{65}(2)=1.
\]
The proposed Jacobi expression gives zero.

**2b is correct for Blum semiprimes on an explicitly imposed even-weight kernel.**

Let \(k=|S|\), \(\lambda=c_1d_1^{-1}\pmod n\), and
\[
\eta=[J_n(\lambda)=-1].
\]
Then every relation satisfies \(t_i\equiv\eta\pmod2\). For even \(k\), the correct congruent roots are
\[
X=d_1^{k/2}U,\qquad Y=c_1^{k/2}W,
\]
where \(\prod_SL_1=U^2\) and \(\prod_SL_2=W^2\). Their Jacobi ratio is
\[
J_n(X/Y)
=(-1)^{(\sum_St_i+\eta k)/2}
=(-1)^{\sum_S\lfloor t_i/2\rfloor}.
\]
This also establishes the stated degeneracy corollary **within those hypotheses**.

But “\(K_0\) requires even weight” is not a consequence of ordinary square conditions. For example,
\[
n=77,\quad L_1=x-78y,\quad L_2=x-y,\quad m=1,
\]
and \((a,b)=(82,1)\) give \(L_1=4,L_2=81\). This is an odd-weight square dependency.

**3. CORRECT WITH FIXES. The union bound is valid; several consequences need repair.**

Fix coordinates on the square-class space generated by **all of \(X\)** before sampling. Do not substitute the random rank of the discovered sample for \(C\).

For the first \(M\) accepted independent samples, let \(A\) have columns \(x_i\), and let \(b=(\beta_i)\). Then
\[
\Phi|_{\ker A}=0
\iff b\in\operatorname{rowspan}(A).
\]
Thus
\[
\Pr(\Phi|_{\ker A}=0)
\le\sum_w(1-\delta_w)^M
\le 2^C(1-\delta)^M
\le e^{C\log2-\delta M}.
\]
That is elementary linear algebra plus a union bound. The algorithm need not know the exact coordinates for this **analysis** to be legitimate.

Rejection sampling gives uniform accepted samples when proposals are uniform and acceptance is the fixed smoothness predicate. If duplicates are discarded, the accepted distinct relations are sampled without replacement; the same upper bound remains valid.

However, the bound concerns **degeneracy of the entire discovered kernel**, not failure of one output. For a semiprime, one uniform kernel sample has success probability at least
\[
\frac{1-\varepsilon}{2}.
\]
With \(t\) independent uniform kernel samples, the failure bound becomes
\[
\varepsilon+2^{-t}.
\]

The involution criterion is correct: each preserved-feature pair forces one disagreement with every linear predictor, giving \(\delta_w\ge\delta'/2\). Dixon supplies this when the relation retains the sampled root \(x\), not merely the residue \(x^2\bmod n\).

The cost conclusion needs an actual implementation argument. Lemma M alone does not justify inheriting LV’s early-abort sampling schedule: favorable fruitfulness parameters could have low relation density.

There is a repair that avoids needing a joint density lemma. At the optimum, put
\[
b_0=(8/9)^{1/3}.
\]
Enumerate the whole candidate box for each chosen parameter pair, retain a uniform reservoir of
\[
M=L_n[1/3,b_0+o(1)]
\]
accepted relations, and retain all accepted relations if there are fewer than \(M\). Under Lemma M, \(C/\delta\) has this size. Full enumeration and sparse algebra both cost
\[
L_n[1/3,2b_0+o(1)]
=L_n[1/3,(64/9)^{1/3}+o(1)].
\]
Repeating over a favorable parameter set of probability \(L_n[1/3,-o(1)]\) preserves that constant.

This requires the correct normalization, uniform kernel sampling, and controlled square testing. LV uses generally nonmonic polynomials, leading-coefficient factors, and an additional constant parity column; those details must enter your formulation. [Lee–Venkatesan, algorithm in §3.3, §§4–6](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873)

**4. CORRECT WITH FIXES. The probability calculation is exact; the lattice size assertion is false.**

Conditional on \(\lambda\), the root is uniform on its fiber
\[
r_0\mu_2(\mathbb Z/n).
\]
For odd \(k\), replacing \(r\) by \(rh\) replaces \(Y\) by \(Yh\). Exactly two of the \(2^{\omega(n)}\) possibilities make \(X/Y=\pm1\). Hence
\[
\Pr(\text{fruitful}\mid \lambda,\text{root-independent coins},\,k\text{ odd})
=1-2^{1-\omega(n)}.
\]
This remains true for nonsquarefree odd \(n\). For \(p^2\), it is zero; “\(1/2\) for semiprimes” requires distinct primes.

Condition on choices made independently of the hidden branch—not on the eventual value of \(Y\) or on a selection procedure that inspected that branch.

The false step is
\[
\det\Lambda_\lambda=n
\quad\Longrightarrow\quad
\text{both reduced basis vectors are }O(\sqrt n).
\]
Take \(\lambda=1\). A Gauss-reduced basis is
\[
(1,1),\qquad
\left(\frac{n+1}{2},\frac{1-n}{2}\right).
\]
The second vector has length \(\asymp n\). Adding a bounded multiple of the first does not fix it.

Replace the deterministic bound by a proved probabilistic bound, or reject poorly shaped lattices using a rule depending only on \(\lambda\). Such rejection preserves the conditional hidden-root argument.

Finally, an odd-weight dependency exists exactly when the all-ones functional does not vanish on the square-condition kernel. This is a linear-algebra criterion, not a guarantee that sufficient smooth relations provide such a dependency. Your \(L[1/2]\) conclusion therefore remains conditional on the relation-generation and odd-dependency assumptions.

**5. WRONG as a general barrier; a restricted counting statement is correct.**

Let
\[
Q=|\bigl((\mathbb Z/n)^\times\bigr)^2|
=\frac{\varphi(n)}{2^{\omega(n)}}.
\]
There are at most \(4H^2\) integer pairs with nonzero \(|c|,|d|\le H\). Therefore a family representing every square residue by \(c/d\) must have
\[
H\ge\frac12\sqrt Q.
\]

This bounds the **maximum height over the family**, not every sampled pair. For uniform \(r\), the useful statement is
\[
\Pr\!\left(\lambda\text{ has a representation }c/d
\text{ with }0<|c|,|d|\le H\right)
\le \frac{4H^2}{Q}.
\]
Consequently, for every fixed \(\epsilon>0\),
\[
\Pr(H(\lambda)\le n^{1/2-\epsilon})
\le n^{-2\epsilon+o(1)}.
\]

The subsequent conclusions do not follow:

- Large coefficients do not impose pointwise lower bounds on form values; cancellation and restricted sampling matter.
- Integer size alone proves no lower bound on smoothness-search cost.
- The counting applies to globally uniform roots. Fruitfulness only needs suitable uniformity **within each root fiber**. The distribution across fibers can be highly nonuniform.
- Quasi-uniform sampling over your entire restricted set is sufficient, but stronger than necessary.

Thus the defensible statement is a barrier to **globally uniform root sampling with uniformly small ratio representatives**, not a theorem excluding hidden-multiplier approaches at \(L[1/3]\).

The Vallée comparison is accurate, with a crucial scale distinction: her bound is \(O(n^{2/3})\), vastly larger than \(L_n[2/3,O(1)]\). [Vallée, *Provably fast integer factoring with quasi-uniform small quadratic residues*, STOC 1989, pp. 98–106](https://doi.org/10.1145/73007.73016)

**A. Exact coordinates and the pairing step.**

Yes, the union bound over exact coordinates is legitimate even when the algorithm uses only projections of those coordinates. But the claimed containment
\[
K_{\rm alg}\supseteq K_0
\]
must include **every** algorithmic constraint.

An extra weight-parity constraint can invalidate it. Here is an exact counterexample:
\[
n=77,\quad f=X-78,\quad m=1,
\]
with relations

| \((a,b)\) | \(a-bm\) | \(a-b\alpha\) | Square roots |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| \((82,1)\) | \(81\) | \(4\) | \((9,2)\) |
| \((403,3)\) | \(400\) | \(169\) | \((20,13)\) |

Both square-class vectors are zero. Both singleton root ratios equal \(43\bmod77\), so both are fruitful. Their even-weight combination has roots
\[
180\equiv26,\qquad26\pmod{77},
\]
and is trivial.

Thus \(\Phi\ne0\) on \(K_0\), while it vanishes on the imposed even-weight kernel. Include the constant feature \(1\) in \(x\) if that restriction remains. This changes the distance problem and can change positive \(\delta\) to zero.

Pairing introduces no inherent obstruction when performed correctly. Let \(W\) be a common computational kernel and let
\[
E:W\longrightarrow H,\qquad \ker E=K_0
\]
record the remaining square-class obstruction. Independent uniform samples in a common fiber \(E=h\) have a sum uniform on \(K_0\). Hence a nonzero \(\Phi\) survives with probability \(1/2\).

Alternatively, uniform sampling from \(W\), followed by exact-square rejection, produces uniform samples from \(K_0\), with acceptance probability \(1/|\operatorname{im}E|\). That gives a clean implementation when the residual obstruction group is small.

An arbitrary collision, deterministic kernel output, or pairing across differently restricted spaces does not inherit this conclusion automatically. LV’s exceptional-class pairing appears in its proof of Theorem 2.6. [Lee–Venkatesan, §6](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873)

**B. Lemma M is not presently a routine application of existing technology.**

For QS or degree one, first specify the parameter distribution replacing LV’s growing-degree polynomial distribution. Otherwise that version of Lemma M is undefined.

For a specified distribution, the exact analytic target is
\[
\delta=\frac{1-\max_w
\mathbb E_X(-1)^{\beta+\langle w,x\rangle}}2.
\]
You must exclude near-perfect correlation **uniformly over all assignments of prime-sign twists**. Showing that \(\beta\) itself is balanced is insufficient.

In degree one, the Blum formula converts these into correlations of multiplicative functions on two simultaneously smooth linear-form values. Their prime values involve
\[
i^{[\ell\in T]}(-1)^{w_\ell},
\]
so the problem retains fourth-root information together with arbitrary square-class twists.

In QS, the analogous problem couples \(J_n(x)\), the prime-exponent parities of \(x^2-n\), and smoothness of that polynomial value.

The most promising route is averaging over coefficients or polynomial parameters and estimating these **twisted smooth correlations**. The obstruction is specific: available untwisted smoothness estimates do not control all those twists after simultaneous smoothness conditioning. Ordinary character-sum bounds also do not supply the required uniform estimates in the very short sieve ranges.

A second route would prove expansion of a graph of smooth relations, forcing any almost-perfect predictor to extend to a forbidden global character. Establishing that expansion is itself the missing arithmetic theorem.

I would treat Lemma M as a substantial conjecture. The explicit formula identifies the obstruction; it does not presently remove it.

**C. Prior work: what can actually be attributed.**

- **The subgroup argument has a clear published precedent.** Seysen, *A probabilistic factorization algorithm with quadratic forms of negative discriminant*, **Math. Comp. 48 (1987), 757–780**, p. **777**, immediately before **Proposition 8.6**, explicitly identifies dependencies yielding trivial factorizations as a proper subgroup. **Theorem 8.7** states uniform sparse-kernel sampling. This is the class-group analogue of Claim 1’s mechanism, not your exact general-NFS statement. [Paper, p. 777](https://scispace.com/pdf/a-probabilistic-factorization-algorithm-with-quadratic-forms-4sxy4xmvw9.pdf)

- **BLP §8 concerns square detection.** Buhler–Lenstra–Pomerance, *Factoring integers with the number field sieve*, **LNM 1554 (1993), 50–94**, §8, **“Quadratic Characters”**; **Lemma 8.2, p. 68**, is the random spanning/union-bound lemma. It does not establish your quantitative fruitfulness-distance lemma. [BLP](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~gasarch/TOPICS/factoring/1993_Book_TheDevelopmentOfTheNumberField.pdf)

- **Lenstra–Pomerance’s exact relevant locations are §2 and §10.** *A rigorous time bound for factoring integers*, **JAMS 5 (1992), 483–516**: §2 gives the ambiguous-form/factorization correspondence; **Algorithm 10.1, Step 6**, constructs the ambiguous form; **Theorem 10.3, p. 507**, gives the factoring probability and runtime. These are rigorous class-group results, not Claims 2–5. [Lenstra–Pomerance](https://www.cs.umd.edu/~gasarch/TOPICS/factoring/rigorous.pdf)

- **Schroeppel’s linear sieve is documented, not a fruitfulness theorem of the proposed kind.** Pomerance, *The quadratic sieve factoring algorithm*, **EUROCRYPT ’84, LNCS 209 (1985), 169–182**, pp. **173–174**, discusses Schroeppel’s unpublished analysis and the linear sieve, including its linear-algebra cost problem. [Pomerance](https://math.dartmouth.edu/~carlp/PDF/paper52.pdf)

- **The sampler precedent is Vallée.** The full treatment is *Generation of elements with small modular squares and provably fast integer factoring algorithms*, **Math. Comp. 56 (1991), 823–849**, especially **Theorems 5–6**; the STOC paper linked above states the \(n^{2/3}\) sampling result.

- **Morrison–Brillhart is not an adequate exact citation for these claims.** Their *A method of factoring and the factorization of \(F_7\)*, **Math. Comp. 29 (1975), 183–205**, is the continued-fraction factoring paper. For the precise subgroup and ambiguous-form assertions, cite Seysen or Lenstra–Pomerance.

I found no exact published statement of your general Bockstein formula, hidden-root two-form construction, or proposed universal barrier. That is a search result, not a novelty certification.

The reproducible [exact checks]( /Volumes/git/factoring-connection/codex/scratch/nfsfruit_2/referee_checks.py) passed **1,008 kernel-vector tests** of the general character formula, including unequal local \(2\)-parts, and verify the counterexamples above.

**The single claim most likely to be wrong is Claim 5:** its counting lemma is sound, but its advertised algorithmic barrier exceeds what that lemma proves.
