**Verdict: Claims 1–4 contain sound results but need fixes. Claim 5 is wrong as a general barrier.** The main unresolved issue is quantitative detection of fruitfulness among the relations the algorithm actually uses.

Throughout, “coprime to everything relevant” must explicitly include
\[
\gcd\!\left(n,f'(m)\prod_i z_i\right)=1.
\]
Otherwise the quotient defining \(\Phi\) need not exist. In semiprime statements, require **distinct** primes \(p,q\).

**1. CORRECT WITH FIXES.**

The homomorphism is correct. Its proof needs no Blum condition—and no odd-degree assumption.

Write \(A_i=a_i-b_i\alpha\). If \(I=S\cap T\), square roots satisfy
\[
\sqrt{\prod_{S\triangle T}z_i}
 =\pm\frac{\sqrt{\prod_Sz_i}\sqrt{\prod_Tz_i}}{\prod_Iz_i},
\]
and the corresponding identity holds in \(K\) for the \(A_i\). Reduction makes the overlap denominators agree. Consequently,
\[
\Phi(S\triangle T)=\Phi(S)\Phi(T)
\]
after quotienting by the global sign.

There are exactly two square roots of a nonzero square in \(K\), irrespective of other roots of unity in \(K\). Thus their choice introduces only the sign already being quotiented out.

The integrality assertion is also valid: the algebraic square root is integral, and
\[
f'(\alpha)\mathcal O_K\subseteq\mathbb Z[\alpha].
\]
For odd \(n\), CRT and the prime-power calculation give
\[
\mu_2(\mathbb Z/n)\cong\{\pm1\}^{\omega(n)}.
\]
The displayed identification with \(\mathbb F_2^{\omega(n)-1}\) is generally **noncanonical**.

For uniform sampling from the **whole** \(K_0\), including zero,
\[
\Pr(\text{fruitful})=1-2^{-\operatorname{rank}\Phi}.
\]
Sampling a basis vector, or an arbitrary nonzero kernel vector, does not have this distribution.

The final sentence about LV is too compressed. Nonvanishing on the full relation space does not establish nonvanishing on the sampled relations or on a further restricted kernel. You need the nonvanishing hypothesis on the space actually sampled, together with its sampling and cost guarantees. LV’s published §7 supplies the Blum character formulation; its Conjecture 7.1 cannot simply be imported unchanged into the non-Blum setting. [Lee–Venkatesan, §7, Theorem 2.3 and Remark 7.3](https://arxiv.org/pdf/1805.08873)

A concrete warning about additional restrictions appears under A below.

**2. CORRECT WITH FIXES. The general character formula survives.**

For distinct \(p,q\), your maximal \(2\)-power characters do satisfy
\[
\chi_p(-1)=\chi_q(-1)=-1,\qquad \psi(-1)=1.
\]
This last identity is essential: it makes the omitted signs harmless.

Because the two evaluations of each relation agree modulo \(n\),
\[
\psi(z_i)=\psi(A_i),
\]
so \(2^c\mid t_i\).

For \(S\in K_0\), all total exponents in both complete multiplicative bases are even, and the total torsion signs permit square roots. Therefore, with reductions interpreted in the appropriate localization,
\[
\frac{\psi(\sqrt{\prod_Sz_i})}
     {\psi(\sqrt{\prod_SA_i})}
 =\zeta^{\frac12\sum_{i\in S}t_i}
 =(-1)^{\sum_{i\in S}\beta_i}.
\]
The factors \(f'\) cancel. If the root ratio has local signs \((\varepsilon_p,\varepsilon_q)\), then
\[
\psi(\varepsilon_p,\varepsilon_q)=\varepsilon_p\varepsilon_q.
\]
That is precisely the nontrivial character of
\(\mu_2(\mathbb Z/pq)/\{\pm1\}\). Hence your formula follows.

Odd degree matters here because it ensures \(\mu(K)=\{\pm1\}\), so the stated quotient of the \(S\)-unit group is free. Use the **full** \(S\)-unit lattice, not merely principal-ideal valuation coordinates.

Changing lifts by
\[
\tau_\ell\mapsto\tau_\ell+2^c k_\ell,\qquad
\sigma_j\mapsto\sigma_j+2^c h_j
\]
changes \(\beta_i\) by
\[
\sum_\ell k_\ell e_{i\ell}-\sum_jh_jc_{ij}\pmod2.
\]
Thus the claimed ambiguity is exactly harmless on \(K_0\). More generally, any two linear extensions of the same \(\Phi\) differ by a linear function of the exact square classes.

Two qualifications:

- “Explicit” does **not** establish efficient computability. Computing a complete \(S\)-unit basis and relation coordinates requires a separate cost analysis.
